<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the implementation assembly: "grains, reconcilers, the chart
      bindings". Two reconcilers, two action handlers, one chart binding — and, since #32, ONE GRAIN,
      which is the first grain in any provider's implementation assembly.

      ⚠ THIS IS THE SECOND LOOK CyberCloud.Providers.Sample's .csproj ASKED FOR. That file: "If a
      future provider needs Orleans in its implementation assembly, that is worth a second look before
      the package goes in." Looked at, and the package goes in, for a reason the twelve families before
      this one did not have: an alert rule has to be EVALUATED on a schedule and has to REMEMBER what it
      found. A reconciler is called by the platform when a body changes; nothing calls it once a
      minute, and clause 2 forbids it remembering anything. The platform's only scheduler is an
      Orleans reminder (docs/plan/04 § Reminders), a reminder is registered by a grain, and a grain
      lives in an assembly that references Microsoft.Orleans.Server. So AlertEvaluatorGrain lives
      here, one per workspace, and IAlertEvaluatorGrain says why per workspace.

      ⚠ WHY HERE AND NOT IN A PLATFORM MODULE, WHICH IS WHERE THE SENDING MODULE'S GRAINS WENT.
      module-layering.txt § CyberCloud.Providers.Communication: those grains stayed in
      CyberCloud.Communication because identity reaches them and rule 2 forbids identity reaching a
      provider. Nothing outside this family reaches an evaluator — the reconciler and the handler are
      its only callers — so it is where docs/plan/03 § Providers put a provider's grains in the first
      place. A CyberCloud.Alerting module would have been a fourth project pair, a host wiring line and
      a module-layering entry to carry one grain nobody else names.

      ⚠ WHAT THE GATEWAY GETS FROM THIS, SINCE RULE 5 LETS IT REFERENCE THIS FAMILY'S .Application
      AND .Application REFERENCES THIS. A grain class in a client's dependency closure hosts nothing —
      a client activates no grain — and the gateway already carries Microsoft.Orleans.Server through
      CyberCloud.ServiceDefaults. What the gateway does need from here is GrainAlertControlPlane, over
      its cluster client, because `listInstances` runs on the request path and reaches a grain;
      MonitorApplicationModule registers it in both hosts.

      ⚠ NO KubernetesClient. docs/plan/03 § Assembly graph rules, rule 3: nothing above
      CyberCloud.Kubernetes names a k8s.Models type. Two of the workspace's three objects are core
      kinds that DO have generated models, which makes this the family most tempted to reach for them —
      building a ConfigMap and a Secret as JsonObject rather than as V1ConfigMap and V1Secret is what
      keeps the rule true without anyone having to remember it, and it is also what lets the render
      write its own `kind` (see MonitorWorkspaces.Matches).
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- Grain implementations and [PersistentState] — AlertEvaluatorGrain. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Server"/>
        <!--
          IRemindable, RegisterOrUpdateReminder, TickStatus — docs/plan/04 § Reminders' fifth use,
          alert evaluation. ⚠ The reminder SERVICE is the host's choice (Redis in production,
          in-memory in a test) and is not decided here.
        -->
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Reminders"/>
        <!-- ForTenant, GetKeyWithinTenant, GetTenantId — GrainAlertControlPlane and MonitorGrainDecoder. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Contracts/CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Contracts.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="CyberCloud.Analyzers — see CyberCloud.Core.csproj for why this is not a reference">
        <!--
          CC1003 ([Alias] on every grain-state class) and CC1006 (GrainAlertControlPlane holds an
          IGrainFactory outside a grain and must qualify with ForTenant) are live here now.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../CyberCloud.Analyzers/CyberCloud.Analyzers.csproj"
                          OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                          ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
